===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|κένωσις||emptying}} (see {{m|en|keno-}} and {{m|el|κενό}}), from {{m|grc|κενόειν||to empty}} (with reference to Philippians 2.vii, "But he emptied himself, taking the form of a slave, becoming as human beings are" – New Jerusalem Version).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɪˈnəʊsɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|kenoses}}

# {{lb|en|Christianity}} [[Christ]]'s [[voluntary]] [[abasement]] consisting of [[humanity]] and the [[simultaneous]] [[occultation]] of the Divinity.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Bradford McCall|title=A Modern Relation of Theology and Science Assisted by Emergence and Kenosis|publisher=Wipf and Stock|isbn=9781532642128|page=61|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OkZPDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA61&dq=%22kenosis%22&hl=&cd=4&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22kenosis%22&f=false|text=So then, '''kenosis''' is the general way in which God interacts with the world. It is interesting to ponder that the term '''kenosis''' is most frequently linked to Christ's voluntary renunciation of certain divine attributes in order to identify himself with mankind, as recorded in the Pauline hymn found in Phil 2:5–11.}}

